  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "For a **global upper-upscale positioning strategy**, an owner should typically consider brands with strong international recognition, broad distribution, and a consistent service/product standard. The most commonly cited upper-upscale brand families in the sources are **Marriott, Hilton, Hyatt, IHG, and select global independents/luxury-soft brands** such as **Westin, Sheraton, Le Méridien, Renaissance, Conrad, InterContinental, Kimpton, Hyatt Regency, Grand Hyatt, Park Hyatt, Fairmont, Shangri-La, Kempinski, and W**.[1][3][4][5][12]\n\nA practical shortlist would be:\n\n- **Marriott International**: **Westin, Sheraton, Le Méridien, Renaissance, Autograph Collection**.[3][12]\n- **Hilton**: **Conrad, Curio Collection, Embassy Suites** are cited in upper-upscale positioning discussions, and Hilton’s broad global footprint supports this tier.[2][18]\n- **Hyatt**: **Hyatt Regency, Grand Hyatt, Park Hyatt, Andaz** are commonly placed in upper-upscale or adjacent luxury positioning.[2][3][6]\n- **IHG**: **InterContinental, Kimpton, Hotel Indigo** are described as upper-upscale brands, with **InterContinental** especially relevant for a global strategy.[5][16]\n- **Other global upper-upscale/luxury-soft brands**: **W, Fairmont, Shangri-La, Kempinski, Langham, Conrad, The Luxury Collection, Banyan Tree**.[3][4][14]\n\nIf the goal is specifically **upper-upscale rather than full luxury**, the most defensible global targets are usually **InterContinental, Conrad, Hyatt Regency, Westin, Sheraton, Le Méridien, Renaissance, and Kimpton**, because these are repeatedly associated with the upper-upscale tier across the sources.[1][2][5][12]\n\nIf you want, I can turn this into a **brand-selection matrix** by owner goal, such as:\n- highest global distribution\n- strongest ADR potential\n- best conversion opportunity\n- best fit for mixed-use or resort assets",
